{"task_id": "Mbpp/256", "solution": "import math\ndef _is_prime(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given number is prime.\n    A prime number is a natural number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number <= 1:\n        return False\n    if number == 2:\n        return True\n    if number % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n    for i in range(3, int(math.sqrt(number)) + 1, 2):\n        if number % i == 0:\n            return False\n    return True\ndef count_Primes_nums(num: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of prime numbers strictly less than the given non-negative number.\n    It iterates from 2 up to num-1, checking each number for primality using a helper function.\n    \"\"\"\n    if num <= 2:\n        return 0\n\n    count = 0\n    for i in range(2, num):\n        if _is_prime(i):\n            count += 1\n    return count"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/260", "solution": "def newman_prime(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the nth term of the Newman-Shanks-Williams (NSW) sequence.\n    Based on the problem statement and example, 'n' refers to the index of the sequence term S_n.\n    \"\"\"\n    def _calculate_nsw_sequence_term(k: int) -> int:\n        # Computes the k-th term of the Newman-Shanks-Williams sequence.\n        # S_0 = 1, S_1 = 1, S_k = 2 * S_(k-1) + S_(k-2) for k >= 2.\n        if k == 0:\n            return 1\n        elif k == 1:\n            return 1\n        else:\n            s_prev2, s_prev1 = 1, 1  # S_0, S_1\n            for _ in range(2, k + 1):\n                current_s = 2 * s_prev1 + s_prev2\n                s_prev2, s_prev1 = s_prev1, current_s\n            return s_prev1\n\n    return _calculate_nsw_sequence_term(n)"}
