{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "def find_kth(array1: list[int], array2: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Finds the kth smallest element from two sorted arrays.\"\"\"\n    n1, n2 = len(array1), len(array2)\n    # The design context provides validation for k, but the prompt asks not to raise exceptions for edge cases.\n    # Therefore, omitting the explicit ValueError for k validation as per \"DO NOT add isinstance() checks or raise exceptions for edge cases\".\n    # Assuming k will be valid according to 1 <= k <= n1 + n2 as per original design intention.\n\n    ptr1 = 0  # Pointer for array1\n    ptr2 = 0  # Pointer for array2\n    current_kth_element = -1\n\n    # Iterate k times to find the kth element\n    for _ in range(k):\n        # Compare elements from both arrays and advance the pointer of the smaller element\n        if ptr1 < n1 and (ptr2 >= n2 or array1[ptr1] <= array2[ptr2]):\n            current_kth_element = array1[ptr1]\n            ptr1 += 1\n        elif ptr2 < n2 and (ptr1 >= n1 or array2[ptr2] < array1[ptr1]):\n            current_kth_element = array2[ptr2]\n            ptr2 += 1\n        # If execution reaches here, it implies an invalid k, but per constraints, we don't raise.\n        # This branch would only be hit if k was larger than the total elements,\n        # and both pointers have exhausted their arrays without finding k elements.\n        # Given the constraint, we just let current_kth_element hold the last valid value.\n    return current_kth_element"}
