{"task_id": "Mbpp/622", "solution": "def get_median(list1, list2, size):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the median of two sorted lists of the same size.\n    The median is the average of the two middle elements from the merged list.\n    \"\"\"\n    i = 0\n    j = 0\n    m1 = -1\n    m2 = -1\n    count = 0\n\n    # Iterate through both lists as if merging, until we find the two middle elements.\n    while count <= size:\n        if i == size: # All elements of list1 have been processed\n            m1 = m2\n            m2 = list2[j]\n            j += 1\n        elif j == size: # All elements of list2 have been processed\n            m1 = m2\n            m2 = list1[i]\n            i += 1\n        elif list1[i] < list2[j]:\n            m1 = m2\n            m2 = list1[i]\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            m1 = m2\n            m2 = list2[j]\n            j += 1\n        count += 1\n\n    return float(m1 + m2) / 2.0"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/620", "solution": "def largest_subset(numbers: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the size of the largest divisible subset from a list of numbers.\n    It finds the longest chain where each number is divisible by the previous one.\"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return 0\n\n    # Sort the numbers in ascending order for the dynamic programming approach.\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n    n = len(sorted_numbers)\n\n    # dp[i] stores the length of the largest divisible subset ending with sorted_numbers[i].\n    dp = [1] * n\n    max_size = 1\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            # If sorted_numbers[i] is divisible by sorted_numbers[j],\n            # it can extend the subset ending at sorted_numbers[j].\n            if sorted_numbers[i] % sorted_numbers[j] == 0:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n        max_size = max(max_size, dp[i])\n\n    return max_size"}
