{"task_id": "Mbpp/635", "solution": "def heap_sort(arr: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts the input list `arr` in place using the heap sort algorithm and returns the sorted list.\n    This function orchestrates heap construction and element extraction phases.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    def _heapify(arr: list, n: int, i: int) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Maintains the max-heap property for a subtree rooted at index `i`.\n        It ensures that the parent node is greater than or equal to its children.\n        \"\"\"\n        largest = i  # Initialize largest as root\n        l = 2 * i + 1  # left child\n        r = 2 * i + 2  # right child\n\n        # See if left child of root exists and is greater than root\n        if l < n and arr[l] > arr[largest]:\n            largest = l\n\n        # See if right child of root exists and is greater than current largest\n        if r < n and arr[r] > arr[largest]:\n            largest = r\n\n        # Change root, if needed\n        if largest != i:\n            arr[i], arr[largest] = arr[largest], arr[i]  # swap\n\n            # Heapify the root.\n            _heapify(arr, n, largest)\n\n    def _build_max_heap(arr: list) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Transforms an arbitrary list into a max-heap by applying the `_heapify` operation.\n        It does this from the last non-leaf node up to the root.\n        \"\"\"\n        # Build a maxheap.\n        # The last non-leaf node is at index n // 2 - 1\n        for i in range(n // 2 - 1, -1, -1):\n            _heapify(arr, n, i)\n\n    # Build a max-heap from the input array\n    _build_max_heap(arr)\n\n    # Extract elements one by one from the heap\n    for i in range(n - 1, 0, -1):\n        arr[i], arr[0] = arr[0], arr[i]  # Swap current root with last element\n        _heapify(arr, i, 0)  # Call _heapify on the reduced heap\n\n    return arr"}
